] "\n" [:option {:value "2353"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "2356"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "2359"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "2362"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "2365"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "2368"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "2371"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "2378"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "2385"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "2391"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "2397"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "2403"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "2409"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "2415"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "2424"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "2433"} "1994"] "\n" [:option {:value "2442"} "1998"] "\n" [:option {:value "2453"} "2002"] "\n" [:option {:value "2466"} "2006"] "\n" [:option {:value "923640"} "2010"] "\n" [:option {:value "350452"} "2014"] "\n" [:option {:value "9000815"} "2018"] "\n" [:option {:value "19020635"} "2022"] "\n" [:option {:value "15001055"} "2026"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/519"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Large Hill, Individual, Men" [:small {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "Many of the scores are different than seen in the 1924 Official Report.  There were three judges who gave a score for each jump and the final score was the average of the six judging scores.  We have re-calculated all the scores, checking the math, because of the well-known problem with the Haug/Haugen scores (see results).  There were several other math errors, as noted below.  However, no other changes in final placement occurred.", :data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right"} "1"]]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 4 February 1924"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/24"} "Le Tremplin Olympique, Le Mont"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2350"} "27 from 9 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two jumps, with both scored on distance and form."]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002583"} "Ingolf Hysing Olsen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002651"} "J. Stengl"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TCH.png"}] "TCH"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004080"} "R. Touchon"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Attendance"] [:td {} "2,702"]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "K-Point: 71 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The Norwegian team for the ski jumping event consisted of two jumping specialists, " [:a {:href "/athletes/84245"} "Narve Bonna"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/98216"} "Jacob Tullin Thams"] ", and the two great allrounders, " [:a {:href "/athletes/89391"} "Einar Landvik"] " (considered as Norway’s best style jumper) and the triple gold medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/86509"} "Thorleif Haug"] ". Haug was not considered to be among the four best of Norway’s ski jumpers, but his place on the team was given in honor of his great allround abilities as a skier."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The only serious contender against the Norwegian supremacy was considered to be the Norwegian-born US jumper " [:a {:href "/athletes/86510"} "Anders Haugen"] ", known for his long and daring jumps, but also for his rugged style and unstable landings."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the first round, Thams, with start number three, took the lead with a jump of 49 metres, ahead of Bonna (47,5) and Haugen (49), with Haug lying 4th with 44 metres, with superb style marks. In the second round, Thams produced another 49 m jump, with better style points than in his first. Haugen made the longest jump in the competition, 50 metres, but was again penalized with low style points for his landing. Haug made 44,5 with even better style marks than in first round. Bonna was the last of the favorites to jump, and he equaled Thams’ 49 metres, but was not able to beat his countryman in the total points. Another clean medal sweep for the Norwegians, with gold to Thams, silver to Bonna, and bronze to Haug, with Haugen given 4th place and the fourth Norwegian, Landvik, in 5th."] "\n\n" [:p {} "After the conclusion of the event, some jumpers made an exhibition for the enthusiastic audience, jumping with top speed. " [:a {:href "/athletes/97917"} "Thoralf Strømstad"] ", silver medalist in both in the " [:a {:href "/results/1890"} "50 km"] " and in " [:a {:href "/results/2184"} "Nordic Combined"] ", made 57,5 metres, the longest jump so far in the history of ski jumping. His mark was equaled later in the exhibition by Thams and Bonna."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Almost 40 years later, the aforementioned Strømstad contacted Norwegian ski historian Jacob Vaage, claiming that the points from the ski jumping event for Thorleif Haug had been miscalculated, and that his final points should be behind Haugen’s. Vaage checked the case and had to agree with the 77-year-old Strømstad. In 1974, the " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] " decided to award the bronze medal to Haugen, at that time an elderly gentleman of 86. He was invited to Norway and, at a nice ceremony, Haug’s bronze medal from 1924 was handed over to Haugen by Haug’s youngest daughter. Thorleif Haug himself died already in December 1934, from pneumonia at the age of 40. But Haugen was pleased to meet some of his Norwegian competitors from 1924: Narve Bonna, Einar Landvik and, not to mention, Thoralf Strømstad, the man responsible for justice being made after 40 years."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Jump #1"] [:th {} "Jump #2"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/98216"} "Jacob Tullin Thams"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "18.960"] [:td {} "18.793 (1)"] [:td {} "19.127 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/84245"} "Narve Bonna"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "18.688"] [:td {} "18.375 (2)"] [:td {} "19.000 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/86510"} "Anders Haugen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "17.917"] [:td {} "18.333 (3)"] [:td {} "17.500 (5)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/86509"} "Thorleif Haug"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "17.813"] [:td {} "17.750 (4)"] [:td {} "17.875 (4)"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {} [:a {